{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "si-03-common-bases-missing-exponents",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Common Bases and Missing Exponents",
    "description": "Make bases match first, then let equal powers reveal the missing exponent.",
    "teachingApproach": "Train the eye to replace 81, 27, 9, 8, 4, decimal bases and powers of 10 by one common base. Only after that conversion are exponents compared or combined. Every worked example explicitly checks that the bases really match.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach the common-base habit behind many objective questions: convert 81 and 9 to powers of 3, 64 and 8 to powers of 2, and decimal bases such as 0.4 or 0.2 to matching powers before comparing exponents. Cover missing exponents in products, quotients and powers of powers, reciprocal-base forms such as (5/2)^x = (2/5)^−3, and linear exponent equations after a base conversion. Include powers of 10 in scientific-notation style quotients because the chapter tests the same law there. Learners should never equate exponents until both sides genuinely have the same positive base."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Many missing-exponent questions are not really about solving a mysterious equation.\n\nThey are about noticing that 81 and 9 are both powers of 3, or that 8 and 4 are both powers of 2. Once the bases match, the exponents usually tell the story.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Take 81^2 × 9^3.\n\n81=3^4 and 9=3^2, so the whole expression can speak base 3.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "81^2×9^3=(3^4)^2×(3^2)^3\n=3^8×3^6\n=3^14\n\nThe hard-looking bases disappeared once we chose a common base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
